# Pure Functions & Side Effects

<img src="https://images.code.org/bff91671a8287ee446cd10565f019e5f-image-1769542918474.png" alt="phone with a volunteer app showing 2 opportinies" width="250px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 40px 00px 10px;">

When you work with data in JavaScript, how you write a function matters just as much as what it does. 

Some functions quietly change data behind the scenes. 

Others leave the original data untouched.

### **Scenario: Filtering Roles**

Your volunteer app starts with a list of opportunities. 

When you run a function to filter that list, one of two things happens:
- the original **stays the same**
- it **gets changed** without you meaning to. 

***Use the visualizer below to see exactly what each method does***.

---

## Array State Vizualizer

>**How to use this visualizer:**
> 1. Look at the code — notice the original array and the method being used.
> 2. Try to predict: will the original array change, or stay the same?
> 3. Press **Run this code** and compare the original array to the result.


