## Defining Pure Function and Side Effect 

### **✅ What is a pure function?**
</p>

<div style="background:#f7f8fa; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin-top:18px; font-size:14pt; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">


<p style= "font-size: 14pt";><b>A pure function:</b>
</p>

<ul>
<li style="font-size:14pt";>Always returns the same output for the same input</li>
<li style="font-size:14pt";>Does not change anything outside itself</li>
<li style="font-size:14pt";>Does not modify the original data it uses</li>
</ul>

<p style= "font-size:14pt":>
In other words, when a pure function runs, the original array stays exactly the same.
The function only computes and returns a <b>new value</b>.

</p>

</div>

### **⚠️ What is a side effect?**

<div style="background:#f7f8fa; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; margin-top:18px; font-size:14pt; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">

<p style="font-size:14pt;"><b>A side effect:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li style="font-size:14pt;">Modifies an array that was passed in</li>
  <li style="font-size:14pt;">Changes a global variable</li>
  <li style="font-size:14pt;">Updates the screen</li>
  <li style="font-size:14pt;">Logs to the console</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
Side effects can make code harder to predict because something changes somewhere else.
</p>

</div>








